\path (-1,2) node[above right] {$(-1,2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
At $x=\frac{1}{2}$:
$\begin{aligned}x^{4}&=\frac{1}{16}\\x^{6}&=\frac{1}{64}\end{aligned}$
The higher-power curve stays closer to the $x$-axis and is flatter near its turning point.

At $x=2$:
$\begin{aligned}x^{4}&=16\\x^{6}&=64\end{aligned}$
The higher-power curve rises more sharply further out. Both powers are even, so the same comparison applies on the negative side.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=16mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{curvered}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[black,<->] (-1.7,0)--(1.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,5.1) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,smooth,samples=101,domain=-1.45:1.45,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{(\x)^4});
\draw[curvered,thick,dashed,<->,smooth,samples=101,domain=-1.29:1.29,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{(\x)^6});
\draw[black] (-1,0.08)--(-1,-0.08) (1,0.08)--(1,-0.08);
\path (-1,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$-1$};
\path (1,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$1$};
\path (0,0) node[below left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$0$};
\path (-1.8,6) node[right,text=curveblue] {solid: $y=x^{4}$};
\path (-1.8,5.4) node[right,text=curvered] {dashed: $y=x^{6}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

